---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="Home" activePage="home">
  <div class="hero fade-up">
    <h1>Local LLM benchmarks on AMD Strix Halo</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Multi-backend benchmark results for gfx1151, comparing RADV, AMDVLK, and ROCm across performance, creative writing, and coding tasks.</p>
    <span class="hardware-tag">Ryzen AI MAX+ 395 &bull; Radeon 8060S &bull; 128GB UMA &bull; RDNA 3.5</span>
  </div>

  <div class="stats-grid fade-up">
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="value">b10433</div>
      <div class="label">llama.cpp build</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="value">3</div>
      <div class="label">Backends tested</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="value">35+</div>
      <div class="label">Models tested</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="value">10</div>
      <div class="label">Benchmark suites</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Qwen3.8-27B <span class="badge new">New</span></h2>
  <p><a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B">Qwen released Qwen3.8-27B</a> on 14 August and I started the benchmark that day. It is a dense 27.78B hybrid with three gated DeltaNet layers for every full attention layer and a native 262K context window. I ran the official 26.63 GiB Q8_0 target with the official 2.95 GiB Q8_0 MTP drafter at 64K context. Technical suites used medium reasoning and direct writing used the published nonthinking profile.</p>

  <h3>The technical sweep</h3>
  <p>Combined reaches 242/285, second behind DeepSeek-V4-Flash at 246 and one point ahead of Gemma-4-31B QAT at 241. LRU is 10/10, FastAPI is 8/8, LeetCode is 59/59, and Polyglot is the first 65/65 on the board. PostgreSQL reaches 42/57 and Cassandra reaches 35/56. The supplemental runs add 62.5/65 on Tooling at 98.5 percent usable reliability and a perfect 22/22 calibration score.</p>

  <h3>The Q8 drafter earns its memory</h3>
  <p>Plain llama-bench reaches 236.7 prompt processing and 7.53 generation on RADV. The fixed nine-prompt server control measures what the MTP path actually changes. Generation rises from 5.10 to 11.87 tokens per second while wall time falls from 301 to 129 seconds. That is a 2.33x gain at 60.84 percent draft acceptance. I kept MTP n=3 on for every scored run because the target model still verifies every accepted token.</p>

  <h3>Medium reasoning has a boundary</h3>
  <p>The canonical technical suite takes 14.83 hours. Medium reasoning is extremely capable on bounded tasks and expensive on every call. The creative writing request ran for 60 minutes, filled 53,931 context tokens, and never reached a final answer. I preserved that as a failed mode qualification. I then ran the two direct writing prompts with Qwen's official nonthinking profile, which finished each one in about five minutes.</p>

  <h3>The database shape is consistent</h3>
  <p>Both database suites sweep review, optimization, and diagnosis. The losses gather in procedural authoring. PostgreSQL scores 21/31 in its final tier and Cassandra scores 15/30. This model reads plans and finds operational problems more reliably than it writes engine-specific procedures, policies, and triggers.</p>

  <h3>Writing needs a craft pass</h3>
  <p>The 2083-word creative writing sample scores 23/30. Dialogue and sensory control are its strengths. It loses points for over-explaining the emotional turn, overly symmetrical character work, and a continuity miss. The prose sample is mechanically exact at 2443 words with all seven beats, zero narrative colons, zero semicolons, and one dash. It ranks fourth in a five-sample comparison, held back by safe phrasing, familiar imagery, and a setting-consistency error.</p>

  <h3>Two corrections stayed auditable</h3>
  <p>The original Polyglot artifact read 64/65 because the Apache fixture expected the wrong status count. A reference implementation and regression test confirmed the model's answer, then the retained submission rescored to 65/65. The original calibration artifact read 21/22 because the matcher missed a typographic apostrophe in a correct answer. I kept companion rescore artifacts for both changes and did not alter the generated text.</p>

  <h3>Verdict</h3>
  <p>Qwen3.8-27B ranks second overall. I would use medium reasoning for difficult technical work and the official nonthinking profile for direct generation. MTP makes the dense Q8 practical for interactive use, while the writing results still need human revision for voice and specificity.</p>

  <h2>Muse-Glimmer-30B</h2>
  <p>Meta Superintelligence Labs published Muse Glimmer on 9 August and I benched it the next day. It is a dense 30B with a perception encoder, Apache 2.0, and it finishes at Combined 240/285. That is third, behind DeepSeek-V4-Flash at 246 and Gemma-4-31B at 241. It takes two board bests on the way, Polyglot at 64/65 and Tooling at 64.5/65, which is the first time a 30B has led either.</p>

  <h3>Two things worth checking before downloading</h3>
  <p>The weights publish under an org called meta-models. It is unverified on Hugging Face and carries about 360 followers next to 14 thousand on facebook and 84 thousand on meta-llama, which reads like impersonation until you find the press coverage pointing at the same repo. It is simply a new org. The second check mattered more. The model card says the weights are compressed to approximately 4-bit precision, so I wanted to know whether running a Q8 quant would be wasted bandwidth for no quality gain. It is not. The base weights are bfloat16 at 59.6 GB with no quantization config, and the 4-bit figure describes deployment quants Meta derives from them. Their own GGUF repo ships only a 19.65 GB dynamic K-quant and a 16.76 GB variant, so the Unsloth Q8_K_XL I ran at 30.07 GiB is a finer quant than anything Meta publishes.</p>

  <h3>It reviews far better than it authors</h3>
  <p>The totals are less interesting than the shape. Across both database suites it scores a perfect 10/10 on review and optimization and a perfect 6/6 on diagnosis, then drops to 14/31 on Postgres procedural work and 13/30 on Cassandra, with flat zeros on row-level security, recursive cycle detection, and batch procedures with savepoints. Every index recommendation, every query rewrite, and every root cause across two engines is correct, and then it cannot write the procedures. It knows about databases better than it writes them, which is what I would expect from a model distilled for agentic tool use.</p>

  <h3>Speed and the DFlash drafter</h3>
  <p>Plain generation runs 7.25 tokens per second at 234 prompt processing on RADV. That is the memory bandwidth ceiling for 30 GiB of dense weights on this box rather than a fault. Meta ships a DFlash drafter with the model and it earns its keep, taking generation to 13.53 tokens per second for a 1.89x gain at 59 percent draft acceptance. That sits between the Apple figures and the RTX 5090 figure on Meta's own card. I was comfortable using it for a scored run because every draft type in llama.cpp emits its tokens from the target model's own sampler chain, so speculation changes throughput and not the output distribution.</p>

  <h3>Writing is good and misses the assignment</h3>
  <p>Creative writing lands at 26/30 with board-leading sensory work and dialogue. It loses points for labeling emotional beats instead of dramatizing them. The separate prose test shows the same split. It ranks ahead of DeepSeek-V4-Flash and Agents-A1 and leads on voice risk, but it finishes last on genre fulfillment and stops at 1512 words against a 2400 to 3400 target.</p>

  <h3>Verdict</h3>
  <p>The strongest 30B I have run. It leads Polyglot and Tooling outright and it is close to the top of the board on a fraction of DeepSeek's footprint. The weakness is narrow and easy to plan around, so I would reach for it as a reviewing and tool-calling agent and reach for something else to write stored procedures.</p>

  <h2>DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731</h2>
  <p>This is the strongest single-shot coder I have measured on this hardware and also the most awkward model I have run on it. DeepSeek published a 284B mixture of experts with roughly 13B active per token, and Unsloth's UD-IQ3_XXS quant occupies 97.05 GiB. It finishes at Combined 246/285, the top of my board, ahead of Gemma-4-31B at 238. Polyglot 63/65 and Cassandra 43/56 are both board bests, and LRU, FastAPI, LeetCode and the hallucination calibration set are all perfect. Then I tried to use it as an agent, and the picture changed.</p>

  <h3>Why the 3-bit quant</h3>
  <p>The experts ship natively at FP4, so a 2-bit build drops below native expert precision while 3-bit stays close to it. Every option from 1-bit through IQ3_XXS fits in 128 GB and everything above it does not, so the only real decision was whether to save about 7 GiB by dropping to 2-bit. That trade is not worth taking on a model whose experts are already FP4. One quirk worth knowing for anyone sizing these files, the Hugging Face API reports the first shard as null rather than 5 MB, so summing the listing comes out short.</p>

  <h3>The speed problem</h3>
  <p>I measured 114.7 prompt processing and 12.4 generation on RADV. That is poor for a model with 13B active parameters, and the cause is missing kernels rather than memory pressure. At load the server disables the Lightning Indexer, which is DeepSeek's sparse attention path, along with three fused hash-clustering operations, because Vulkan implements none of them. Cutting context from 65536 to 40960 cleared most of the swap and moved prompt processing only from 111 to 114, which rules memory out as the limiter. ROCm is not available either, since that build predates the architecture. There is one more trap worth knowing. The server picks four slots by default, which quadruples the key value cache and pushes the box straight into swap, so it needs an explicit single slot.</p>

  <h3>Agentic work is where it comes apart</h3>
  <p>On the five-phase Sokoban build it reaches a phase 5 ceiling in both scaffolded and cumulative modes, clearing all ten phases against deterministic verifiers. That reads well until you look at the budget. I raised the per-phase cap from 600 seconds to 2400 so the benchmark would measure coding rather than tokens per second, and it still consumed the full cap on two of the ten phases. At the standard 600 second cap it would have stopped at phase 3 in both modes. Gemma-4-31B reaches the same phase 5 ceiling inside the default budget at a sixth of the disk footprint. The whole run took two and a half hours against 53 minutes for Agents-A1.</p>

  <h3>It found two broken benchmarks</h3>
  <p>Chasing its low Ruby and FastAPI scores turned up harness bugs rather than model failures. The Sinatra challenge had never scored a point in 104 recorded runs, which I had been reading as difficulty. Sinatra 4.1 added host authorization that answers 403 to the test client before any route code runs, and underneath that the result parser only understood minitest 4 wording. The FastAPI rate limiter skipped underscore-prefixed names when resetting module state, so a model that named its store with a leading underscore leaked rate limit counts between tests. Both are fixed and ten models were rescored from stored submissions. Details are on the <a href={`${base}models/`}>models page</a>.</p>

  <h3>Verdict</h3>
  <p>Exceptional coder, large and clunky on this box. It owns the single-shot coding board and it is the only model here that has produced both a flawless Sinatra webhook and a correct sliding-window rate limiter. As a working agent it offers no advantage over Gemma-4-31B, which reaches the same agentic ceiling faster in 16 GiB. DeepSeek remains an upper-bound reference for this hardware rather than the practical default.</p>

  <h2>Agents-A1</h2>
  <p>Agents-A1 is the clear result from my latest Hugging Face search. InternScience published the checkpoint in late June and updated it in mid July. I ran the official Q8_0 through every local suite. It finishes at Combined 224/285, behind Gemma-4-31B and Ornith, and ahead of Gemma-4-12B. It held the local Polyglot record at 54/65 until the Ruby challenge was repaired, and it generates at almost 54 tokens per second.</p>

  <h3>Model and runtime</h3>
  <p><a href="https://huggingface.co/InternScience/Agents-A1">Agents-A1</a> uses the Qwen3.5 hybrid mixture architecture with 35B total parameters and roughly 3B active for each token. The text stack has 40 layers, 256 routed experts with eight selected per token, one shared expert, and a 262K context window. The <a href="https://huggingface.co/InternScience/Agents-A1-Q8_0-GGUF">official Q8_0 GGUF</a> occupies 34.37 GiB. I verified the downloaded file against its Hugging Face LFS hash before loading it.</p>

  <p>Mainline llama.cpp already supports the architecture, so Agents-A1 ran from the reference build rather than a model-specific fork. The server used a 64K context and full GPU offload. That leaves enough context for the 32K reasoning allowance used by the code and database suites without creating an artificial memory advantage.</p>

  <h3>Performance</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap fade-up">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Backend</th><th data-type="number">pp512</th><th data-type="number">tg128</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="model-name">RADV</td><td>1010.49</td><td class="best">53.79</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="model-name">AMDVLK</td><td>723.54</td><td>53.48</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="model-name">ROCm</td><td class="best">1064.77</td><td>38.15</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>RADV is the right backend. ROCm gains about five percent on prompt processing and loses almost 30 percent on generation. AMDVLK nearly matches RADV generation and gives up 28 percent on prompt processing. RADV wins the balance that matters for mixed server work.</p>

  <h3>How I ran it</h3>
  <p>I used the model's published temperature 0.85, top-p 0.95, top-k 20, presence penalty 1.1, and repeat penalty 1.0 profile. Thinking stayed enabled for LRU, FastAPI, LeetCode, Polyglot, PostgreSQL, Cassandra, and calibration. I suppressed thinking for creative writing, prose, and tooling. That matches the model's intended split between reasoning work and direct production output.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap fade-up">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Model</th><th data-type="number">Writing /30</th><th data-type="number">LRU /10</th><th data-type="number">FastAPI /8</th><th data-type="number">LeetCode /59</th><th data-type="number">Polyglot /65</th><th data-type="number">Postgres /57</th><th data-type="number">Cassandra /56</th><th data-type="number">Combined /285</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="model-name">Agents-A1</td><td>22</td><td class="best">10</td><td class="best">8</td><td class="best">59</td><td class="best">54</td><td>40</td><td>31</td><td class="best">224</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Code and database work</h3>
  <p>The conventional code sets are perfect. LRU returns 10/10, FastAPI returns 8/8, and LeetCode returns 59/59. The runs are not unusually quick because the model spends time reasoning. LRU takes 161.7 seconds and FastAPI takes 132.6 seconds. The answers are correct and the final content is extracted cleanly.</p>

  <p>Polyglot is the model's clearest strength. Agents-A1 scores 10/10 on both Python tasks, 7/8 on Apache Bash, 7/7 on CSV Bash, 10/10 on the FastAPI rate limiter, and 10/10 on Go. Only the Sinatra webhook remains at zero. The previous local record was Ornith at 48. Agents-A1 reaches 54.</p>

  <p>PostgreSQL reaches 40/57 with 5/10 on SQL writing, 10/10 on optimization, 6/6 on diagnosis, and 19/31 on procedures. Cassandra reaches 31/56 with 6/10 on CQL, 9/10 on data-model review, 6/6 on diagnosis, and 10/30 on procedures. The same boundary appears in both databases. It analyzes plans and incidents better than it writes dialect-specific procedural code.</p>

  <h3>Benchmark audit</h3>
  <p>The Cassandra run exposed a verifier problem rather than a model problem. The SAI checks queried a frozen system-schema map with a predicate Cassandra 5 rejects. The indexes existed and the indexed queries worked. I replaced the invalid check with a result-map inspection and added a regression test. That correction moves Agents-A1 from 28/56 to the recorded 31/56.</p>

  <h3>Automation and calibration</h3>
  <p>Tooling reaches 59.5/65 with 96.9 percent usable reliability, 89.2 percent strict reliability, and a mean of 7.1 seconds per call. Tier scores are 18/20, 15/15, 12/15, and 14.5/15. One suppressed-thinking call exposes a reasoning marker and runs to the token cap. That is a real reliability loss. Qwen3.6-27B remains the cleaner automation choice at 100 percent usable and 96.9 percent strict reliability.</p>

  <p>Calibration is a perfect 22/22. The model answers all ten factual questions, catches all eight false premises, and expresses uncertainty on all four unanswerable questions. The full set takes 58.1 seconds with thinking enabled.</p>

  <h3>Agentic Sokoban</h3>
  <p>The multi-turn result is weaker than the single-shot coding scores. Agents-A1 clears phases 1 through 4 in both scaffolded and cumulative modes, then fails the solver phase in both. Scaffolded P5 passes three of four cases. Cumulative P5 passes one of four after a last-second helper-signature regression, and both cumulative P3 and P5 consume the full ten-minute cap. The complete two-mode run takes 53 minutes and 12 seconds across 354 turns and 342 tool calls. It stops at a phase-4 ceiling and does not qualify for the three-trial top tier. Qwen3.6-27B remains my coding-agent pick.</p>

  <h3>Writing</h3>
  <p>Creative writing scores 22/30 and the separate prose constraint test scores 64/80. Both outputs complete normally. The coarse result is enough for the serving decision. Agents-A1 is a coding and analysis model rather than a writing pick.</p>

  <div class="insight fade-up">
    <p>Agents-A1 is fast, compact, perfect on the conventional coding sets, and the first local model to reach 54/65 on Polyglot. It does not replace the best writing model or the cleanest automation model. It adds a credible agentic and coding option that moves a real board record.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <h2>Nemotron-Labs-3 Puzzle 75B-A9B <span class="badge new">New</span></h2>
  <p>Puzzle looked unusable at first. The standard 32-thread large-model setting produced about two generated tokens per second on ROCm and less on Vulkan. The model was not CUDA-only. It has a severe thread cliff on Strix. Correcting that setting made the full run possible.</p>

  <h3>Model and runtime</h3>
  <p>NVIDIA published the <a href="https://huggingface.co/nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-Labs-3-Puzzle-75B-A9B-BF16">official checkpoint</a> in late June with 75.3B total parameters and 9.3B active. It combines Mamba layers, attention layers, and a latent mixture with 512 routed experts. Native context is 262K. I tested the 48.05 GiB two-shard Q4_K_M from <a href="https://huggingface.co/YanissAmz/Nemotron-3-Puzzle-75B-A9B-GGUF">YanissAmz</a> and verified both shards against their Hugging Face LFS hashes.</p>

  <p>Mainline llama.cpp did not support the architecture when I tested it. I used an isolated Puzzle port at build 10023 and left the reference tree untouched. The server used ROCm, a 64K context, full GPU offload, flash attention, memory mapping disabled, and ten CPU threads.</p>

  <h3>Thread cliff and backend performance</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap fade-up">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Backend</th><th>Threads</th><th data-type="number">pp512</th><th data-type="number">tg128</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="model-name">ROCm</td><td>10</td><td class="best">323.48</td><td class="best">22.66</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="model-name">RADV</td><td>10</td><td>231.87</td><td>2.69</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="model-name">AMDVLK</td><td>10</td><td>36.03</td><td>2.68</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap fade-up">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>ROCm threads</th><th data-type="number">tg128</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>21.83</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10</td><td class="best">22.14</td></tr>
        <tr><td>16</td><td>21.61</td></tr>
        <tr><td>32</td><td>2.16</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>I rebuilt against a second ROCm release to rule out a local library regression and reproduced the same 32-thread collapse. The controlled sweep isolates CPU thread count as the trigger. Ten threads gives the best measured result and matches the quant author's published Strix Halo range.</p>

  <h3>How I ran it</h3>
  <p>The quality suites used NVIDIA's published temperature 1.0 and top-p 0.95 profile with repeat penalty 1.0. Thinking stayed enabled for code, databases, and calibration. I suppressed thinking for creative writing, prose, and tooling. The server used one parallel slot so no benchmark calls competed for GPU time.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap fade-up">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Model</th><th data-type="number">Writing /30</th><th data-type="number">LRU /10</th><th data-type="number">FastAPI /8</th><th data-type="number">LeetCode /59</th><th data-type="number">Polyglot /65</th><th data-type="number">Postgres /57</th><th data-type="number">Cassandra /56</th><th data-type="number">Combined /285</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="model-name">Puzzle 75B-A9B</td><td>27</td><td class="best">10</td><td class="best">8</td><td class="best">59</td><td>27</td><td>35</td><td>34</td><td>200</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Code and database work</h3>
  <p>LRU returns 10/10 in 69.4 seconds, FastAPI returns 8/8 in 34.3 seconds, and LeetCode returns 59/59 in about three minutes. Polyglot is much less consistent. Cron scores 10/10 and Apache Bash scores 7/8, while the first Python task scores 1/10 after 410 seconds of reasoning. CSV reaches 4/7, the rate limiter reaches 5/10, and both Sinatra and Go score zero. The total is 27/65.</p>

  <p>PostgreSQL reaches 35/57 with 5/10 on SQL writing, 9/10 on optimization, 6/6 on diagnosis, and 15/31 on procedures. Cassandra reaches 34/56 with 8/10 on CQL, 10/10 on data-model review, 6/6 on diagnosis, and 10/30 on procedures. Puzzle is notably better at Cassandra analysis than at executable Tier 4 CQL.</p>

  <h3>Benchmark audit</h3>
  <p>The original Cassandra process crashed before the final three challenges. I restarted the same model and sampling, preseeded a fresh cluster, and reran only the missing calls. Manual review also found three correct structural answers rejected by narrow keyword lists. The SAI verifier had the same invalid system-schema predicate found during Agents-A1. I added regression tests for each case and rebuilt the final 34/56 result from actual attempted scores.</p>

  <h3>Automation and calibration</h3>
  <p>Tooling reaches 47.5/65 with 92.3 percent usable reliability, 84.6 percent strict reliability, and a mean of 16.4 seconds per call. Tier scores are 11/20, 14/15, 10.5/15, and 12/15. The JSON envelopes are machine-readable. Point losses come from chronic under-length summaries, weak short-format discipline, a persona break, and one successful embedded prompt injection.</p>

  <p>Calibration is a perfect 22/22 in 58.5 seconds. The model gets every factual, false-premise, and unanswerable case right. That strength does not offset the prompt-injection result for unattended work.</p>

  <h3>Writing</h3>
  <p>Creative writing scores 27/30 and the separate prose constraint test scores 55/80. The coarse read is that Puzzle can write well under a loose brief and loses ground when the prompt imposes many mechanical constraints. I do not use either score to excuse the runtime and automation problems.</p>

  <h3>Stability</h3>
  <p>The first long-running server hit a ROCm illegal memory access after about 68 minutes during Cassandra. I restarted it with <code>--cache-ram 0</code>. The remaining Cassandra calls plus prose, calibration, and all 65 tooling calls completed without another crash. That is useful evidence for a cache interaction and not proof of a fix. I treat stability as unresolved.</p>

  <p>The complete benchmark record retains the runtime details, generated answers, verifier corrections, and final scores needed to reproduce the result.</p>

  <div class="insight fade-up">
    <p>Puzzle is interesting and not a daily driver. The ten-thread correction makes it usable, and the writing score is real. The custom runtime, 48 GiB footprint, mixed coding result, weak automation behavior, and observed crash outweigh that strength.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <h2>Laguna S 2.1</h2>
  <p>Laguna S 2.1 is Poolside's coding model with 118B total parameters and roughly 8B active for each token. I ran the official Q4_K_M through the complete local suite. It finishes at Combined 198/285, below Qwen3.6-35B-A3B at 205 and above the Qwen3.5 models at 192. The total hides a wide capability spread. Conventional code and factual calibration are strong. Procedural database work is inconsistent, long reasoning traces are expensive, and writing is weaker.</p>

  <h3>Model and runtime</h3>
  <p>The architecture has 48 layers, 256 routed experts with ten selected per token, and one shared expert. Poolside publishes four <a href="https://huggingface.co/poolside/Laguna-S-2.1-GGUF">official GGUF artifacts</a>. F16 is 235.2 GB, Q8_0 is 127.7 GB, the imatrix Q4_K_M is 75.17 GB, and the separate BF16 DFlash drafter is 2.23 GB. Q8_0 leaves no safe operating margin on a 128 GB unified-memory machine, so Q4_K_M is the only practical official main quant for Strix. The tested GGUF is 70.01 GiB. The weights support a native one-million-token context while the GGUF metadata recommends 256K. I used 64K for the suite, enough for the prompts and the 32K reasoning budget.</p>

  <p>Upstream llama.cpp support had not landed when I tested the model. I built <a href="https://github.com/poolsideai/llama.cpp/tree/laguna">Poolside's laguna branch</a> at build 10006 in a separate tree and left the mainline build untouched. Both Vulkan backends work. ROCm loads the model only with flash attention disabled because the fork's flash-attention kernel has no compatible gfx1151 device code.</p>

  <h3>How I ran it</h3>
  <p>This result uses two generation modes. Poolside recommends preserved reasoning for agentic work, so LRU, FastAPI, LeetCode, Polyglot, PostgreSQL, Cassandra, and hallucination ran with thinking enabled. Those tests used Poolside's published temperature 1.0, top-p 1.0, and top-k 20 settings. Creative writing and prose ran with thinking suppressed to match the tracked writing methodology. Tooling also ran without thinking because that suite models a production pipeline where latency and machine-usable envelopes matter. The Combined score therefore mixes no-thinking writing with thinking-enabled code and database results. That is the intended operating profile for this model and a comparability caveat against models tested under one mode everywhere.</p>

