  <p>RADV is the serving backend because generation matters more than prompt processing for the tracked workloads. ROCm wins pp512 but gives up 12 percent on tg128 and cannot use flash attention. Laguna's 21.63 tg is respectable for a 70 GiB file, though it trails smaller MoE models such as Qwen3.6-35B-A3B at 59.6 tg.</p>

  <h3>Code and database work</h3>
  <p>LRU, FastAPI, and LeetCode all return perfect scores. Polyglot reaches 35/65 on a single run, tied with the best of five Qwen3.6-35B-A3B draw and behind Ornith at 48 and Gemma-4-31B at 44. Laguna earns full points on CSV aggregation, the sliding-window FastAPI limiter, and the Go rule engine. It takes partial credit on both Python tasks and fails the Apache Bash and Sinatra challenges. The Go and rate-limiter solves make the result more useful than the Combined rank alone suggests.</p>

  <p>The database scores show a consistent boundary. PostgreSQL earns 7/10 on SQL, 9/10 on optimization, 5/6 on diagnosis, and 8/31 on procedural work. Cassandra earns 6/10 on CQL, 9/10 on data-model review, 6/6 on diagnosis, and 15/30 on procedural work. Laguna understands faulty plans and schemas better than it writes executable procedures. LeetCode is perfect but sometimes slow. Median of Two Sorted Arrays takes 389.5 seconds and Minimum Window Substring takes 358.9 seconds, with roughly 7.7K reasoning tokens in each trace.</p>

  <p>Hallucination calibration is a clean 22/22 in 16.9 seconds. Tooling scores 57.5/65 and resists all three prompt-injection trials, but four JSON-envelope calls are unusable. That leaves usable reliability at 90.8 percent and makes Qwen3.6-27B the safer unattended-automation model.</p>

  <h3>Writing</h3>
  <p>Creative writing scores 21/30 and the separate prose constraint test scores 53/80. Both outputs complete normally. The coarse result is enough for the serving decision. Laguna is stronger at code and diagnosis than writing.</p>

  <h3>DFlash</h3>
  <p>I tested Poolside's official DFlash drafter instead of assuming speculative decoding would help. Across nine fixed prompts, the plain model predicts 1,586 tokens in 83.70 seconds at 18.95 tokens per second. DFlash predicts 1,582 tokens while drafting 8,890 candidates and accepting 955. Acceptance is 10.74 percent, wall time rises to 218.37 seconds, and throughput falls to 7.24 tokens per second.</p>

  <p>The fixed-seed long-form comparison is worse. The plain run produces 3,214 tokens in 149.47 seconds at 21.8 tokens per second. DFlash produces 2,099 tokens in 337.36 seconds at 6.27 tokens per second, accepting 976 of 16,830 drafted tokens. Acceptance falls to 5.8 percent and the output terminates 1,115 tokens earlier, so the speculative path does not preserve the baseline distribution in this test. The retained benchmark record is sufficient to reject DFlash on this hardware.</p>

  <div class="insight fade-up">
    <p>Laguna remains useful on record as a coding and diagnosis specialist. The 35/65 Polyglot result, 36/56 Cassandra score, and perfect calibration are credible strengths. Its 70 GiB footprint, expensive reasoning tails, weaker writing, and 90.8 percent tooling reliability keep it from replacing the faster daily drivers. I would serve Qwen3.6-35B-A3B for routine interactive work and consider Laguna when its Go, rate-limiter, or database-analysis profile matches the task.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <h2>Ornith-1.0-35B</h2>
  <p>Ornith-1.0-35B remains one of the most interesting results on the board. It is DeepReinforce's reinforcement-learning fine-tune of Qwen3.5-35B-A3B aimed squarely at agentic coding, released late June 2026 under an MIT license. At Q8_0 it lands Combined 227/285, second overall behind Gemma-4-31B at 238, and it is an always-on reasoning model so I ran the whole suite thinking-on. What makes it worth a spotlight is the comparison built into its own family. I have the base Qwen3.5-35B-A3B on record at 192 and a different agentic fine-tune, Nex-N2-mini, at 183. Ornith clears both by a wide margin, and because I ran it the same thinking-on way I ran Nex, the 44-point gap between them is a clean measure of what good reinforcement training does to a base.</p>

